              "text": "Grid.lean — the sealed source"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "json",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"Grid.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/Grid.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 6,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-decimal_asks_three",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE BASE-TEN RULE: 6w carries digital root nine exactly when w is a multiple of three, since the decimal digit-sum invariant is mod 9 and gcd(6,9) = 3. Wings three at a time — a consequence of writing in ten."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((List.range 40).all (fun w => ((6 * w) % 9 == 0) == (w % 3 == 0))) ∧ ((6 * 91) % 9 ≠ 0)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem decimal_asks_three : ((List.range 40).all (fun w => ((6 * w) % 9 == 0) == (w % 3 == 0))) ∧ ((6 * 91) % 9 ≠ 0) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-hexadecimal_asks_five",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE BASE-SIXTEEN RULE: the hexadecimal digit-sum invariant is mod 15 (since 16 = 1 mod 15), so 6w vanishes there exactly when w is a multiple of FIVE. The same grid, the same six rays, a different demand — and the addresses this ledger computes are written in sixteen."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((List.range 40).all (fun w => ((6 * w) % 15 == 0) == (w % 5 == 0))) ∧ ((6 * 72) % 15 ≠ 0)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem hexadecimal_asks_five : ((List.range 40).all (fun w => ((6 * w) % 15 == 0) == (w % 5 == 0))) ∧ ((6 * 72) % 15 ≠ 0) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-seventytwo_is_decimal_only",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "AND THE SEALED WIDTH SATISFIES ONE BASE ONLY: 6 x 72 = 432 leaves 0 mod 9 and 12 mod 15. The count the grid was fitted to is harmonic in decimal and unremarkable in hexadecimal, which the line proves rather than leaves to be noticed."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(6 * 72 = 432) ∧ (432 % 9 = 0) ∧ (432 % 15 = 12) ∧ (432 % 15 ≠ 0)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem seventytwo_is_decimal_only : (6 * 72 = 432) ∧ (432 % 9 = 0) ∧ (432 % 15 = 12) ∧ (432 % 15 ≠ 0) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-reversal_fails_in_hexadecimal",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE FUSION IS DECIMAL SPELLING, shown in the base the addresses use: 72 is 0x48, its hex reversal is 0x84 = 132, and 16 x 132 = 2112 rather than 432. The identity that fused the two factorisations holds for one spelling in one base and nowhere else."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(16 * 27 = 432) ∧ (16 * 132 = 2112) ∧ (16 * 132 ≠ 432)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem reversal_fails_in_hexadecimal : (16 * 27 = 432) ∧ (16 * 132 = 2112) ∧ (16 * 132 ≠ 432) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-fifteen_satisfies_both",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE BASE-AGNOSTIC RULE: a wing count divisible by FIFTEEN satisfies decimal and hexadecimal at once, because lcm(3,5) = 15. Such a rule holds whatever base a reader writes in, which is the only kind worth gating on — and 90 is the first count in range that meets it."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((List.range 40).all (fun w => (w % 15 == 0) == (((6 * w) % 9 == 0) && ((6 * w) % 15 == 0)))) ∧ (90 % 15 = 0) ∧ (91 % 15 ≠ 0)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem fifteen_satisfies_both : ((List.range 40).all (fun w => (w % 15 == 0) == (((6 * w) % 9 == 0) && ((6 * w) % 15 == 0)))) ∧ (90 % 15 = 0) ∧ (91 % 15 ≠ 0) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-six_rays_stay_derived",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "AND WHAT SURVIVES UNTOUCHED: the six is derived. Seven dimensions less the identity ray, because projecting a wing into the language it is already written in computes nothing — 7 x 72 - 72 = 432 = 6 x 72. The multiplier was never the problem; only the constant it was multiplied to."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(7 * 72 - 72 = 432) ∧ (6 * 72 = 432) ∧ (7 - 1 = 6)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem six_rays_stay_derived : (7 * 72 - 72 = 432) ∧ (6 * 72 = 432) ∧ (7 - 1 = 6) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
